      <div class="space-y-4 text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          In bovine milk, cream, and industrial sweet buttermilk, lipids exist as natural oil-in-water emulsions stabilized by a delicate 10 to 20 nm trilayer biological coating: the <strong>milkfat globule membrane (MFGM)</strong>. This membrane consists of polar phospholipids (phosphatidylcholine, phosphatidylethanolamine, sphingomyelin) and embedded glycoproteins (butyrophilin, lactadherin, xanthine oxidase). In conventional dairy processing, separating fat from serum requires either high-speed rotating disk-stack centrifuges (which consume massive mechanical energy, generate friction heat, and cause irreversible shearing of casein micelles) or thermal heating (&gt;65°C), which denatures valuable whey immunoglobulins and accelerates lipid rancidity through auto-oxidation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>Commercial Continuous High-Intensity Acoustic Shockwave Hydrodynamic Defatting (ASH-DD)</strong> process demulsifies fat globules without heat or chemical surfactants via three coupled biophysical phenomena:
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-muted">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-ink">Rayleigh-Plesset Cavitation Dynamics:</strong> High-frequency alternating acoustic pressure waves (20–45 kHz, acoustic intensity &gt;180 W/cm²) drive cyclic tensile stress that nucleates micro-bubbles during the rarefaction half-cycle. Under 15 to 35 bar static pressure, these bubbles collapse asymmetrically within 2.5 µs, launching localized liquid micro-jets at velocities exceeding 1,150 m/s with localized shear rates &gt;10<sup>7</sup> s<sup>−1</sup>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-ink">Selective MFGM Corona Cleavage:</strong> These transient hydrodynamic micro-jets physically strip the outer hydrophilic glycoprotein layer from the lipid core without hydrolyzing fatty acid ester bonds. Stripped neutral triacylglycerols rapidly coalesce into larger macro-droplets (d_4,3 &gt; 15 &mu;m), while intact polar sphingomyelins and phospholipids remain solvated in the aqueous serum phase.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-ink">Acoustophoresis &amp; Hydrocyclone Density Fractionation:</strong> Standing acoustic wave radiation forces accelerate droplet migration into acoustic pressure nodes, while downstream sanitary micro-hydrocyclones (3,200 × g) partition the coalesced cream overflow from the ultra-skim underflow (&lt;0.04% residual fat) at ambient temperature (18–25°C) with zero whey protein denaturation.
          </li>
        </ol>
